  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1122 AM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nFor the rest of the day, a tightened pressure gradient is creating\nbreezy west-northwest winds. Observed gusts of 25 to 35 mph \ntoday will continue into this afternoon, but they are expected \nto decrease late this afternoon into the supper hours early this\nevening. Calm conditions are expected by early tonight for \nnearly all locations. The one exception will be downsloping \nwinds remaining mixed out for terrain funneled areas of the \nNorth Shore where winds will linger late this evening. There \nwill also be a few rain showers along the Borderlands through \nlate afternoon today under the cyclonic cold air advection \nassociated with the deep low pressure over far northern Ontario.\n\nA weak warm air advection regime sets up Thursday daytime into \nThursday night leading to seasonable warm to almost hot \ntemperatures both days. Expect mild and dry conditions through \nFriday mid-day.\n\nBy Friday early afternoon, severe weather chances begin to first\nincrease for north-central Minnesota as an advancing cold front\nstemming from a low pressure over central Manitoba shifts into the\nUpper Midwest. Global ensemble guidance is progging a moist airmass\naround 1.5 inches of precipitable water lining up with favorable\nmid-level lapse rates and instability profiles. This supports an\nuptick in thunderstorm development likely early to mid-afternoon,\nfirst in the Brainerd Lakes to Leech Lakes region. By later\nafternoon as better shear moves into the region and CAPE values\ncontinue to indicate a growing availability of energy for\nstorm growth, the more likely time period for severe weather \nsets up into northeast Minnesota for late afternoon to the early\nto mid evening hours. The stateline counties of northwest \nWisconsin right along the Minnesota and Wisconsin border would \nalso be included in this greatest severe weather threat for \nFriday evening when damaging wind gusts and large hail are \npossible. Locally heavy rainfall is also expected under this \nenvironmental setup and we will have to watch for possible minor\nflooding Friday evening. Severe weather chances will taper late\nFriday evening to early overnight as the cold front and \ntroughing move through the region. Expect a downturn in shower \nactivity from west-northwest to east-southeast Friday night.\n\nGusty northwest winds return for Saturday daytime under cooler\ntemperatures as high pressure builds in behind the departing cold\nfront. For Saturday late night into Sunday early morning, far\nnorthern Minnesota likely sees temperatures dipping into the upper\n30s. We will have to monitor for any mid 30s potential as well in\nthe usual cold spots near the Iron Range and into the Arrowhead up\ninto Koochiching County.\n\nSunday afternoon into Monday, a warm front sets up south of the\nregion into southern Minnesota and central Wisconsin to create\ngusty easterly onshore winds for all lakeshore areas. This pattern\nalso favors a more fall-like cloudy and longer duration light to\nmoderate rainfall with embedded general thunderstorm potential for\nMonday daytime. While the rainfall ends by Tuesday daytime,\nfall-like near-normal to cooler temperatures and more breezy\nconditions will persist into the middle of next week.",
